  <p class="sa-masthead-note">&dagger; Work done during an internship at Sakana AI.</p>

  <figure class="figure fig-mid historical-teaser" id="historical-teaser">
    <a class="teaser-image" href="assets/figures/printing-workshop-c1600.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Open the printing workshop engraving at full resolution">
      <img src="assets/figures/printing-workshop-c1600.jpg" srcset="assets/figures/printing-workshop-c1600-small.jpg 599w, assets/figures/printing-workshop-c1600.jpg 3547w" sizes="(min-width:800px) 752px, calc(100vw - 48px)" width="3547" height="2707" decoding="async" fetchpriority="high" alt="Engraving of a printing workshop around 1600: workers set type on the left, operate a press on the right, and lay out printed sheets for proofreading in the foreground.">
    </a>
    <figcaption><b>Figure 1. From shared work to shared knowledge.</b> This engraving shows typesetting, printing, and proofreading as parts of a coordinated process. Books carry the result beyond the workshop, where others can learn from it and build on it. MASS draws on a related idea: a team's collective work can provide training examples for the model its members share.
      <span class="image-credit">Jan Collaert I, after Stradanus, <i>The Invention of Book Printing</i>, c. 1600. <a href="https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/659683" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Metropolitan Museum of Art</a>, Harris Brisbane Dick Fund, 1934. <a href="https://www.metmuseum.org/hubs/open-access" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Public domain (CC0)</a>.</span>
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <!-- ===================== INTRODUCTION ===================== -->
  <!-- <p id="introduction">We want frontier AI models to take us beyond the body of knowledge humanity has built over generations. At that frontier, even experts may struggle to assess the results and guide further progress<dt-cite key="burns2023weak"></dt-cite>. A proposed discovery may demand scarce expertise, costly evidence, or experiments that take far longer than generating the proposal. The ability to produce new work will advance faster than our ability to supervise it.</p>

  <p>This motivates <strong>homogeneous recursive self-improvement</strong>, in which the model being improved (<em>optimizee</em>) also serves as its own optimizer and evaluator. A stronger teacher may be unavailable, and human experts may be unable to provide reliable feedback at the pace or depth required. The model itself may then be the most capable supervisor available. The central question is how to obtain useful self-supervision within this homogeneous loop.</p> -->
  <p id="introduction">We want frontier AI models to take us beyond the body of knowledge humanity has built over generations.
    At that frontier, even human experts may struggle to assess results and guide further progress, particularly on non-verifiable tasks<dt-cite key="burns2023weak"></dt-cite>.
    For example, evaluating a proposed discovery may require scarce expertise, costly evidence, or experiments that take far longer than generating the proposal. The model itself may then inevitably be the most capable supervisor available and need to figure out for itself how to improve.
    This motivates <strong>homogeneous recursive self-improvement (RSI)</strong>, in which the model being improved (<em>optimizee</em>) also serves as its own optimizer and evaluator.
    The central question is how to obtain useful self-supervision within this homogeneous loop.</p>

  <p>Human societies offer a useful analogy for addressing this question. Across foraging communities, farming settlements, industrial societies, and global information networks, people have expanded their capabilities in part by dividing work, combining ideas, and passing on what they learn<dt-cite key="muthukrishna2016collective,derex2016partial"></dt-cite>. Collective achievements become a starting point for further learning. In <em>The Structure of Scientific Revolutions</em>, Thomas Kuhn emphasized the shared examples through which scientists learn to recognize and solve problems<dt-cite key="kuhn1970structure"></dt-cite>. A scientific community's achievements thus become part of how its members learn to do new work.</p>

  <p>Inspired by this pattern, we ask whether <strong>a model can learn from its own team's collective intelligence</strong>, which an agent working alone may not reliably exhibit.</p>

  <p>We introduce <strong>Multi-Agent Self-Supervision (MASS)</strong> to turn this idea into a learning loop. MASS uses the same model as the executor, optimizer, and evaluator. The optimizer first searches for the best team workflow using feedback from the evaluator, then trains the executor on executions from the self-judged best team. The updated model then fills all three roles, and the process repeats. Therefore, MASS provides <strong>a self-supervision signal for RSI by distilling the team's collective experience into the shared model</strong>.</p>

  <!-- ===================== SETTING ===================== -->
  <h2 id="roles">Homogeneous RSI</h2>

  <p>We study homogeneous RSI, in which one shared language model serves as the task-solving agent, workflow optimizer, and evaluator:</p>

  <div class="mw roles">
    <div class="role-card" id="role-agent">
      <p class="role-name">Agent<button type="button" class="fig-ref" data-fig="executor">↓ in figure</button></p>
      <p class="role-desc">The task solver, or <em>optimizee</em>. Produces a workspace containing results, plots, and a research report.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="role-card" id="role-optimizer">
      <p class="role-name">Workflow optimizer<button type="button" class="fig-ref" data-fig="optimizer">↓ in figure</button></p>
      <p class="role-desc">Uses the feedback from the evaluator to revise how agents divide the task, exchange results, and check one another's work.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="role-card" id="role-evaluator">
      <p class="role-name">Evaluator<button type="button" class="fig-ref" data-fig="evaluator">↓ in figure</button></p>
      <p class="role-desc">Compares two workspaces, chooses which better satisfies the task, and explains the judgment.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>We use non-verifiable research tasks as training data. We use <em>non-verifiable</em> to mean that an automatic rule-based signal is not available and that only a self-evaluation signal (whose correctness is unknown) guides the overall learning loop.</p>

  <p>To measure progress, we separately ask GPT-5.5 and Claude Opus 4.8 to assess the completed work. Their judgments are withheld from MASS. This gives us an outside measure of improvement in a process whose supervision comes from the model itself.</p>

  <!-- ===================== METHOD ===================== -->
  <h2 id="how-mass-works">How MASS works</h2>

  <p>MASS uses workflow optimization to elicit collective intelligence,
    then distills the team's experience into the shared model.
    The <span class="fig-term" data-fig="inner">inner loop</span> optimizes the team's workflow while the model weights remain fixed. The model evaluates and judges for itself which workflow is optimal.
    The <span class="fig-term" data-fig="outer">outer loop</span> then updates those weights by learning from executions of the optimized workflow. <a class="fig-term" href="#mass-figure" data-fig="all">The interactive diagram below</a> follows both loops.</p>

  <figure class="figure sa-hero mh-figure" id="mass-figure">
    <div class="mh-scroll">
    <svg class="mh-svg" viewBox="0 0 960 580" role="group" aria-label="Interactive diagram of recursive self-improvement with MASS. Each part links to its explanation in the text.">
      <defs>
        <symbol id="mh-cube" viewBox="0 0 40 42">
          <polygon points="20,1.5 38.5,10.5 20,19.5 1.5,10.5" fill="#FBE4E3" stroke="#B85450" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
          <polygon points="1.5,10.5 20,19.5 20,40.5 1.5,31.5" fill="#F5CBC9" stroke="#B85450" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
          <polygon points="20,19.5 38.5,10.5 38.5,31.5 20,40.5" fill="#EFB9B6" stroke="#B85450" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
        </symbol>
        <symbol id="mh-cube-bold" viewBox="0 0 40 42">
          <polygon points="20,1.5 38.5,10.5 20,19.5 1.5,10.5" fill="#F7D3D1" stroke="#A13E3A" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
          <polygon points="1.5,10.5 20,19.5 20,40.5 1.5,31.5" fill="#EDB2AF" stroke="#A13E3A" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
          <polygon points="20,19.5 38.5,10.5 38.5,31.5 20,40.5" fill="#E39C98" stroke="#A13E3A" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
        </symbol>
        <marker id="mh-ah" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0.5 L10,5 L0,9.5 z" fill="#2b2b2b"/></marker>
        <marker id="mh-ah-s" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="5" markerHeight="5" markerUnits="userSpaceOnUse" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0.5 L10,5 L0,9.5 z" fill="#9a9aa3"/></marker>
        <marker id="mh-ah-g" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0.5 L10,5 L0,9.5 z" fill="#9a9aa3"/></marker>
      </defs>

      <!-- ===== inner loop ===== -->
      <g class="mh-hit mh-region" data-key="inner" data-target="#inner-loop" tabindex="0" role="button"
         data-tip="Inner loop: the model evolves its own team workflow by running it, judging the result, and rewriting it."
         aria-label="Inner loop: jump to its explanation">
        <rect class="mh-region-box" x="12" y="12" width="936" height="330" rx="22"/>
